# Secure SQL

Created: 2026-11-04T21:45:23Z (first recorded addition in Git)
Last updated: 2026-11-10T15:55:25Z

## Purpose and applicability

Support intentional SQL execution while protecting data, credentials, resources,
and access boundaries.

These principles apply across project languages and SQL engines.
Coursework follows its applicable course instructions.

## Application-generated queries

When constructing SQL, keep external values separate from query structure.
Use the selected engine's supported parameter-binding or typed expression
mechanisms rather than interpolating values into SQL text.

Data previously read from storage remains external input when used in another query.

Dynamic identifiers, operators, and other structural choices require validation
against the permitted schema or supported choices. Use appropriate engine-specific
identifier handling; value parameters do not substitute for identifiers.

Reject unsupported structural input explicitly. Do not strip suspicious characters
and assume the resulting query is safe.

For pattern matching, distinguish intentional wildcard behavior from literal text.
Parameter binding does not change wildcard semantics.

## User-submitted SQL

Accepting SQL is an intended engine capability. Treat it as an explicit execution
interface with defined permissions.

Successful parsing or planning does not establish that an operation is authorized.

Define which datasets, statements, functions, file locations, network destinations,
and extension capabilities are available as those features are introduced.

Do not let query text automatically grant access to arbitrary resources or
privileged operations. Use established engine and application controls rather than
attempting to secure arbitrary SQL with keyword filtering.

Keep authorization appropriate to the implemented interface. This rule does not
require a speculative service or user-account system.

## Resource ownership and enforcement

The application or deployment owns maximum resource budgets.
User-supplied SQL or request settings must not raise or disable those ceilings.

An interface may allow callers to request lower limits within the permitted budget.

Introduce relevant controls as execution capabilities develop, such as memory
budgets, deadlines or cancellation, concurrency limits, and bounded external I/O.

Verify what each control actually enforces. Engine-level limits may cover only
part of process memory or execution; document material gaps and use external
enforcement when stronger isolation is required.

A result row limit is not a general bound on scanning, sorting, aggregation,
memory use, or execution time.

Report exhausted budgets and cancellation explicitly. Do not present interrupted
or partial execution as a complete successful result.

## Data and diagnostics

Preserve original data and respect the staged-data and output rules in
[pipeline_io.mdc](pipeline_io.mdc).

Keep sensitive query text, literals, and parameter values out of routine logs.
Use safe identifiers and summaries under
[logging_and_errors.mdc](logging_and_errors.mdc).

Distinguish successful empty results, null values, execution failures,
and incomplete results.

## Verification and evolution

Add focused checks when introducing query construction, resource controls,
or access boundaries. Verify relevant rejection and failure behavior as well
as successful execution under [testing.mdc](testing.mdc).

Do not assume an engine feature is available or enforced without checking the
selected version and integration.

Follow [security.mdc](security.mdc) for shared security principles and
[coauthoring_protocol.mdc](coauthoring_protocol.mdc) for ownership and scope.
